></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="16" rect="595.276001,636.879761,779.276001,728.879761"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250630093304+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250630093321+10'00'" name="21703192-298f-4dee-8218-9cc448b0e9d3" page="16" coords="32.825195,728.879761,283.905731,728.879761,32.825195,719.084473,283.905731,719.084473,32.825195,718.448364,283.881531,718.448364,32.825195,708.653076,283.881531,708.653076,32.825195,707.960388,283.894348,707.960388,32.825195,698.165100,283.894348,698.165100,32.825195,697.528992,87.757675,697.528992,32.825195,687.733704,87.757675,687.733704" rect="32.519089,687.427612,284.211853,729.185852" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>No key biomechanical variable associated with PT could be&#xD;replicated between studies. Knee flexion angle at IC and hip&#xD;extension rather than flexion strategy during a horizontal land of a&#xD;vertical stop-jump land were the only two variables associated&#xD;with asymptomatic PTA that could be replicated between-studies.&#xD;Key differences in jump-landing biomechanics may be conditional&#xD;to specific jump-landing tasks and/or observed cohorts.&#xD;Experimental task selection should include a horizontal landing as&#xD;isolated vertical landings and/or take-offs alone may not be sensitive&#xD;enough to identify key jump-landing variables associated with</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
> </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>PT. Clinicians should focus their assessment of jump-landing&#xD;technique in those with PT or at risk of developing PT on sagital&#xD;plane hip and knee kinematics, particularly during the horizontal&#xD;landing phase.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="16" rect="595.276001,636.879761,779.276001,728.879761"
/></highlight
></annots
><f href="10.1016_j.ptsp.2020.03.011.pdf"
/><ids original="6353A8D328A95FB246FA970293867311" modified="F0CCB68A6884944D8149769F90BC3DBA"
/></xfdf
>